/** @page developer-guide Developer guide

@section environment Environment

@subsection cmake-presets CMake presets

To setup a local build configuration, for example if you are using VSCode's
CMake integration, create a CMake user preset that in `CMakeUserPresets.json`
in the root directory of the project. This user preset can inherit from one of
the standard ones in `CMakePresets.json` and add its own variables. For
example:

@code{.ansi}
{
  "version": 3,
  "configurePresets": [
    {
      "name": "macos-local",
      "displayName": "macOS local",
      "generator": "Ninja",
      "inherits": [
        "macos-debug"
      ],
      "cacheVariables": {
        "CMAKE_PREFIX_PATH":  "/Users/jamie/llvm-project/install/bin",
        "CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE": "Debug",
        "ENABLE_PY_BINDINGS": "On",
        "CODE_COVERAGE": "Off",
        "BUILD_DOCS": "Off"
      }
    }
]
}
@endcode

Then to use this preset:

@code{.ansi}
cmake --preset macos-local
cmake --build build/macos-local --target install
@endcode

To build the documentation install the Python dependencies:

@code{.ansi}
virtualenv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
# Compile with -DBUILD_DOCS=ON
@endcode

@subsection pre-commit Pre commit

[Pre-commit](https://github.com/pre-commit/pre-commit) runs a set of lint
checks on files when creating git commits, with the config in `.pre-commit`.

You can install the hooks explicitly with:

@code{.ansi}
pre-commit install
@endcode

And you can run `pre-commit` checks on all files with:

@code{.ansi}
pre-commit run -a
@endcode

To update the versions of the hooks used in the `pre-commit` configuration file:

@code{.ansi}
pre-commit autoupdate
@endcode

@subsection ubuntu-docker Ubuntu Docker environment

You can use Docker to create an Ubuntu development environment, eg:

@code{.ansi}
docker build -t slang-netlist-dev docker/ubuntu
docker run -it slang-netlist-dev
@endcode

This is useful to debug issues seen in CI, especially when developing on MacOS.

@section testing Testing

@subsection unit-tests Unit tests

To run the unit tests:

```sh
ctest --test-dir build/<preset-name> --output-on-failure --no-tests=error
```

Report code coverage:

```sh
# Compile with -DCODE_COVERAGE=ON, then:
make ccov-run-netlist_unittests
make ccov-report
```

Profile performance using cachegrind:

```sh
valgrind --tool=cachegrind netlist_unittests
# Analyse results in speedscope.
```

@subsection external-tests External tests

The test suite includes tests using code from external projects, enabled with
`-DENABLE_EXTERNAL_TESTS=ON` (this is the default).

**RTLmeter** is a collection of large reference designs including
BlackParrot, Caliptra, NVDLA, OpenPiton, OpenTitan, Servant, VeeR, Vortex,
XiangShan and XuanTie; sourced from
[verilator/rtlmeter](https://github.com/verilator/rtlmeter). It is fetched via
CPM at configure time and each design is elaborated through the full netlist
construction pipeline.

To run only the external tests:

@code{.ansi}
ctest --test-dir build/macos-local -R rtlmeter-tests --output-on-failure
@endcode

The RTLmeter suite runs one unittest per design; failures print the
`slang-netlist` stderr so the root cause is visible directly in the test
output. Each design has a 5-minute timeout and the overall test has a 1-hour
timeout. Because these are large, real-world designs, some may fail due to
slang-netlist limitations and are useful for tracking coverage progress.

To skip the external tests entirely (e.g. for faster iteration):

@code{.ansi}
cmake --preset macos-local -DENABLE_EXTERNAL_TESTS=OFF
@endcode


@section architecture Architecture

The library builds a directed dependency graph (the "netlist") over an
elaborated SystemVerilog AST provided by
<a href="https://sv-lang.com">slang</a>. The graph captures source-level
static connectivity at bit-level granularity. The main components are
described below.

@subsection arch-graph Graph data structures

The graph is built on a generic @c DirectedGraph<NodeType,EdgeType> template
that stores nodes in an adjacency list of @c std::unique_ptr<NodeType>.
Each edge is single-owned by its source node via @c std::unique_ptr<EdgeType>
in @c outEdges; the target node holds a raw back-pointer in @c inEdges. A
per-node @c outEdgeIndex (a @c NetlistNode*&nbsp;→&nbsp;@c NetlistEdge* map
used to find the first edge to a target) is allocated lazily only once a
node's out-degree exceeds @c outEdgeIndexThreshold, so low-fan-out nodes pay
no per-node map overhead. The netlist specialises this as @c NetlistGraph,
holding @c NetlistNode and @c NetlistEdge objects.

@c NetlistNode is a polymorphic base with a @c NodeKind discriminator.
Concrete subtypes are:

- @c Port: an input or output port of a module instance.
- @c Variable: a net or variable declaration.
- @c State: a register (variable driven inside a clocked procedural block).
- @c Assignment: a continuous or procedural assignment expression.
- @c Conditional / @c Case: an if or case branch in a procedural block.
- @c Merge: a synthetic join point that merges two branches back together.
- @c Constant: a literal or constant-foldable RHS (including the
  zero-extension bits of a widening conversion). Carries the
  @c ConstantValue and the bit width it drives, and acts as a leaf
  source feeding the consuming Assignment or Port.

@c NetlistEdge carries annotations for the driven symbol, its bit range, and
an @c ast::EdgeKind that records clock sensitivity (used to distinguish
combinational from sequential edges).

Since one edge describes a single symbol over a single contiguous range, a
node pair connected by several symbols, or by disjoint ranges of one symbol,
is represented by parallel edges. Adding a dependency therefore reuses an
existing edge only when it can absorb the new annotation (same symbol, same
edge kind, and a range contiguous with the stored one), and creates a parallel
edge otherwise.
Phase 5 below then collapses the ranges that arrived out of order.

Two operations express this. @c addEdge always creates an edge, so the caller
owns its annotation; @c getOrAddEdge returns the first existing edge to the
target, so it suits callers that carry no annotation or have checked that the
existing edge can absorb theirs. Writing an annotation onto a reused edge
destroys the previous one.

@subsection arch-construction Graph construction

@c NetlistBuilder is the main AST visitor (@c slang::ast::ASTVisitor).
The five-phase construction is orchestrated by the @c BuildPipeline class,
which @c NetlistBuilder owns:

<b>Phase 1: Collect (sequential).</b> @c BuildPipeline::runPhase1 traverses
the AST sequentially via @c root.visit(builder). During this phase the
@c collectingPhase flag is set, so @c handle() methods for
@c ProceduralBlockSymbol and @c ContinuousAssignSymbol do not execute the DFA
immediately. Instead, they push their symbols onto the @c deferredBlocks work
list.
All other @c handle() methods run normally, creating @c Port, @c Variable,
and instance-structure nodes, registering port connections, and populating the
@c VariableTracker.

<b>Phase 2: DFA dispatch (parallel or sequential).</b> Each entry in
@c deferredBlocks is dispatched as an independent task. In parallel mode
@c BuildPipeline::runPhase2Parallel detaches the tasks to a
@c BS::thread_pool; in sequential mode @c runPhase2Sequential calls the
same per-block helpers in a simple loop.

Each task runs a local @c DataFlowAnalysis that computes reaching definitions
for all variables referenced in the block (see @ref internals-dfa). On
completion the task calls @c mergeDrivers to fold per-block driver intervals
into the central @c ValueTracker. Nodes and edges created during the DFA are
added directly to the shared @c NetlistGraph (per-node @c edgeMutex on
edge insertion, single @c nodesMutex on @c addNode, see
@ref arch-multithreading).

Tasks complete in an arbitrary order, so whatever a task contributes to
shared state has to be order-independent. Folding driver intervals is,
because @c ValueTracker::addDrivers unions them
(@c DriverUpdate::Merge). The one range that is not a plain union is a
clocked block's @c State node, which has to displace the placeholder
registered for the declaration in Phase 1 while leaving drivers
contributed by other blocks alone (@c DriverUpdate::Supersede).

Work that cannot be made order-independent is deferred instead, and
accumulates in a thread-local @c DeferredGraphWork buffer:

- <b>Pending R-values</b>: operands whose full set of drivers is not yet
  known.
- <b>Variable hookups</b>: a driver's connection to the node standing for
  a variable's storage. The lookup has to wait, because the task that
  creates that node may still be running.

<b>Phase 3: Drain (sequential).</b> After all Phase 2 tasks have completed,
@c PendingRvalueQueue::drain collects the thread-local buffers into one
queue per kind of deferred work, freeing each per-task buffer as it is
consumed to keep peak memory down. Buffers are drained in @c deferredBlocks
order rather than completion order, so the queues themselves are
deterministic.

<b>Phase 4: R-value resolution (parallel or sequential).</b>
@c BuildPipeline::finalize() calls @c PendingRvalueQueue::resolve.

That first resolves the deferred variable hookups: every node now exists,
so @c resolveVariableHookups looks each one up and connects the driver to
it. The lookup is exact-bounds, so a hookup whose range does not match a
node's exactly finds nothing and is dropped; the driver still reaches its
readers through the driver-interval walk below.

It then iterates over every pending R-value and connects it to its
driver(s) in the graph. For each pending entry, @c emitEdgesFor walks the
driver intervals that overlap the pending range (via
@c ValueTracker::forEachDriverInterval)
and emits an edge per driver, annotated with the precise sub-range the
R-value actually reads. Reads resolve only this way, which is what keeps
those sub-ranges exact: a @c State or @c Variable node covering the whole
range is itself recorded as a driver of it, so taking a single edge to such
a node whenever one existed would hide any block co-driving part of the
range. When the queue size exceeds
@c BuilderOptions::parallelRValueThreshold (1000 by default) and
@c parallel is true, @c resolveParallel groups entries by target node so
each target's incoming edges are emitted from a single thread. Grouping is
done in place: the queue is sorted by target pointer and a run-start vector
indexes each contiguous run, avoiding an
@c unordered_map<NetlistNode*,vector<size_t>> that could cost many MB of
transient overhead on large designs.

<b>Phase 5: Merge parallel edges (sequential).</b>
@c BuildPipeline::finalize() then calls @c NetlistGraph::mergeParallelEdges,
which sorts each node's outgoing edges into (target, symbol, edge kind,
range) order and greedily unions contiguous ranges. An edge carries a single
contiguous range, so a driver/reader pair covering a disjoint set of bits is
necessarily several edges; building that set incrementally is sensitive to
the order the ranges arrive in, which is not stable across runs. This phase
restores the canonical form: one edge per maximal contiguous range. Only
edges sharing all four attributes are combined and unannotated edges are
left alone, so connectivity is unchanged. Only nodes that received a
parallel edge during construction are visited.

Node ordering, node IDs and the order of each node's edges still follow
construction order, so JSON and DOT output is not byte-comparable between
runs; only the edge set itself is canonical.

Key helper classes (each lives in its own translation unit under @c source/):

- @c BuildPipeline: drives the five-phase build, owns @c deferredBlocks,
  and records timings into @c BuildProfile.
- @c NodeFactory: single point of construction for @c Port, @c Variable,
  @c State, @c Assignment, @c Conditional, @c Case, @c Merge, and
  @c Constant nodes, keeping @c VariableTracker and the per-node bookkeeping
  in sync.
- @c PendingRvalueQueue: owns the queues of work deferred out of Phase 2
  (pending R-values and variable hookups) and implements both the
  sequential and parallel resolution paths.
- @c PortConnectionHandler: materialises port nodes, records cuts from
  concat-shaped actuals, and emits port-connection edges.
- @c CanonicalBodyResolver: maps non-canonical instance bodies and their
  value symbols to their canonical counterparts so each instance of a
  multi-instantiated module gets its own per-bit routing (see
  @ref internals-canonical-bodies).

Other key supporting types:

- @c ValueTracker / @c VariableTracker: interval-map-based structures that
  track which netlist nodes drive which bit ranges of each symbol.
- @c DriverMap: a thin wrapper around slang's @c IntervalMap, mapping bit
  ranges to driver lists.
- @c ExternalManager<T>: a handle-based allocator used because
  @c IntervalMap values must be trivially copyable.
- @c SymbolTable: per-graph intern table for @c SymbolReference, so every
  edge holds a pointer to a single shared entry rather than a copy of the
  name / hierarchical path / location.
- @c CutRegistry: side table mapping formal ports' internal symbols to
  the bit offsets at which external concats split them; consulted by
  port-node creation and by @c BitSliceList::pushLsp to keep paths
  through concat-shaped port connections bit-precise.

@subsubsection arch-multithreading Multithreading

Phase 2 of netlist construction is parallelised using a
<a href="https://github.com/bshoshany/thread-pool">BS::thread_pool</a>
(bundled with slang). During Phase 1 (the sequential collecting pass),
procedural blocks and continuous assignments are not executed immediately but
are instead pushed onto a @c deferredBlocks work list. In Phase 2 each
deferred block is dispatched as an independent task to the thread pool, where
it runs its own @c DataFlowAnalysis and merges results back into the shared
graph.

Thread safety is achieved through several mechanisms:

- The slang AST is frozen (@c Compilation::freeze()) before Phase 2, making
  it safe for concurrent reads. The Python bindings then call
  @c Compilation::unfreeze() before @c NetlistGraph.build so the builder can
  continue elaborating; @c slang-netlist freezes only for the analysis pass
  and runs its own sequential @c VisitAll before that.
- @c DirectedGraph::addNode is serialised by a single @c nodesMutex. Adding
  an edge touches both endpoints' edge lists, so it holds the @c edgeMutex of
  each. The two are acquired together rather than in a fixed
  source-then-target order, since a fixed order deadlocks when two threads
  add reciprocal edges between the same pair of nodes: Phase 4 partitions
  pending R-values by target node, so that pairing arises whenever two blocks
  each read what the other writes.
- @c ValueTracker and @c VariableTracker use per-slot locking, so updates to
  distinct symbols do not contend. A @c ValueTracker's slot-indexed vectors
  sit behind a reader-writer lock because growing them can reallocate, so
  the tracker that several threads share grows them in a way that does not
  need exclusive access once per symbol (@c SlotGrowth).
- Pending R-values and variable hookups are accumulated in thread-local
  @c DeferredGraphWork buffers during Phase 2 and merged sequentially in
  Phase 3, so no mutex is required around either queue.
- Anything a Phase-2 task reads from shared state must already be complete,
  or the result depends on task ordering. @c VariableTracker is written in
  both Phase 1 and Phase 2, so a Phase-2 lookup of an entry that Phase 2
  also creates is unsafe; such lookups are deferred to Phase 4 instead (see
  @c VariableTracker::lookup for the precise rule and its one exception).
- @c NetlistNode IDs are allocated with an atomic counter.

@subsection arch-analysis Analysis and queries

- @c PathFinder: finds a path between two nodes using depth-first search;
  returns a @c NetlistPath.
- @c CombLoops / @c CycleDetector: detects combinational loops by only
  traversing edges without clock sensitivity (@c EdgeKind::None).
- @c DepthFirstSearch: generic DFS template parameterised on a visitor and
  an edge predicate; used internally by @c PathFinder, @c CycleDetector,
  and @c NetlistGraph's fan-in/fan-out queries. Header lives under
  @c source/ rather than @c include/netlist/ since it is not part of the
  public API.

@subsection arch-tooling Tooling

- @c tools/driver/driver.cpp: the @c slang-netlist CLI binary. It links
  against the @c netlist library and exposes commands such as
  @c --report-registers, @c --comb-loops, @c --from/@c --to path queries,
  @c --netlist-dot graph export, and the @c --black-box pattern flag
  (see @ref internals-black-boxes).
- @c bindings/python/pyslang_netlist.cpp: nanobind Python module
  (@c pyslang_netlist); enabled with @c -DENABLE_PY_BINDINGS=ON. Built in
  nanobind's split mode against the same @c nanobind_backend runtime as
  pyslang, so slang objects pass between the two extensions.

@subsection arch-deps Dependencies

All fetched automatically via CPM at configure time:

- <a href="https://sv-lang.com">slang</a>: SystemVerilog
  compiler/AST/analysis (pinned to a specific git hash).
- <a href="https://github.com/fmtlib/fmt">fmt</a>: string formatting.
- <a href="https://github.com/catchorg/Catch2">Catch2</a>: unit testing
  framework.
- <a href="https://github.com/wjakob/nanobind">nanobind</a>: Python
  bindings, fetched by slang. Requires the @c nanobind-backend package at
  runtime.


@section internals Internals

@subsection internals-dfa Data flow analysis

The @c DataFlowAnalysis pass extends slang's @c AbstractFlowAnalysis to
compute <b>reaching definitions</b> for each variable at every program point
within a procedural block. A reaching definition records <em>which netlist
node(s)</em> last wrote to each bit range of a variable. This is richer than
slang's built-in @c DefaultDFA, which only tracks <em>whether</em> a bit range
has been driven (for diagnostic purposes such as detecting use-before-assign),
without recording what drove it.

Reaching definitions are maintained in the @c AnalysisState as
@c ValueDrivers, a per-symbol interval map from bit ranges to lists of
@c DriverInfo (a netlist node paired with the AST expression that produced
it). When an R-value reference is encountered, the analysis looks up the
current reaching definitions for that symbol and adds edges from the defining
nodes to the current node. When an L-value reference is encountered, the
reaching definitions are updated to record the new definition. Non-blocking
assignments (@c <=) are deferred and applied at the end of the procedural
block via @c finalize().

Conditionals whose condition carries a pattern match (@c matches) are walked
here rather than by the base class, which visits the pattern after the flow
state has already split into true and false branches. A pattern visit rejoins
a split state, so visiting one there discards both branches, taking with them
the current node and every definition made earlier in the block. Both the
statement and expression forms therefore read the condition and its pattern
while the state is whole, then fork and rejoin the two arms explicitly. The
match itself stays opaque: both arms are taken to contribute. For the same
reason such a condition is never treated as constant, since evaluating it
yields the subject rather than the result of the match.

@subsection internals-graph Graph structure

Nodes represent operations or state, and edges represent data dependencies.
An edge points from producer to consumer, i.e. data flows from the source
node to the target node.

<b>Node types:</b>

- @c Port: a module port (input or output), annotated with direction,
  hierarchical path, and bit bounds.
- @c Variable: a declared variable (wire, reg, logic, etc.), annotated with
  hierarchical path and bit bounds.
- @c State: the persistent value of a register: created whenever a
  variable is driven on a clocked edge inside a procedural block, so
  combinational consumers downstream see the registered value rather than
  the raw procedural drivers. It replaces the declaration's placeholder
  driver for the range, but coexists with drivers from other blocks that
  write the same range, such as an @c initial block initialising the
  register.
- @c Assignment: a continuous or procedural assignment operation.
- @c Conditional: an @c if branch point within a procedural block.
- @c Case: a @c case branch point within a procedural block.
- @c Merge: a synthetic join point where control flow from branches
  reconverges.
- @c Constant: a literal or constant-foldable RHS, including the
  zero-extension bits of a widening conversion. Carries the
  @c ConstantValue and the bit width it drives, and acts as a leaf
  source feeding the consuming @c Assignment or @c Port.

<b>Edge annotations:</b>

- @c symbol: the @c SymbolReference identifying the driven variable.
- @c bounds: the @c DriverBitRange indicating which bits are carried by the
  dependency.
- @c edgeKind: @c ast::EdgeKind (e.g. @c PosEdge, @c NegEdge, @c None)
  recording clock sensitivity, used to distinguish combinational from
  sequential edges.
- @c disabled: flag used to exclude edges during analysis (e.g. by
  @c CycleDetector).

@subsection internals-bit-aligned Bit dependency resolution

When @c BuilderOptions::resolveAssignBits is true (the default),
@c DataFlowAnalysis::handle(AssignmentExpression) and
@c NetlistBuilder::handlePortConnection decompose both sides of the
assignment or connection into a @c BitSliceList and zip them onto a common
cut-point grid via @c alignSegments. The <em>cut-point grid</em> is the
sorted union of every slice boundary on either side: the union is taken so
that each resulting segment falls entirely within a single slice on the LHS
and a single slice on the RHS, and so that operands can therefore be
matched bit-for-bit. Each segment becomes its own @c Assignment node, with
the LHS driven from the LSPs that fall within the segment and RHS edges
added only from LSPs that actually contribute to those bits.

@c BitSliceList::build (in @c source/BitSliceList.cpp) walks an expression
and recognises four structural kinds; everything else collapses to a single
@c BitSliceSource::Kind::Opaque slice covering the expression's
@c getSelectableWidth():

- @c Concatenation: operands are appended in reverse so that the LSB
  operand has @c concatLo @c == @c 0.
- @c Replication: only when the count is a constant non-negative
  @c int64_t. A zero-count replication produces no slices.
- @c Conversion: equal-width conversions pass through; widening
  conversions emit the operand's slices first followed by a
  @c BitSliceSource::Kind::Padding slice for the extension bits, which
  produce no edges. Narrowing conversions cannot be represented in a
  pass-through slice list and fall back to opaque.
- @c ConditionalOp (@c \?:): only when both arms and the result have the
  same selectable width and the condition has no pattern. Both arms are
  re-segmented onto a shared cut-point grid and the condition expression is
  attached as an opaque source on every unified slice so that it shows up
  as a dependency of every bit.

For port connections @c NetlistBuilder::buildPortSliceList builds the formal
side from the @c Port nodes already registered for the symbol (one or more
@c BitSliceSource::Kind::PortNode sources per segment, depending on how the
port was split, since an inout port has both an input-side and an output-side
node at overlapping ranges) and aligns it against the actual-side slicelist
the same way.

@subsection internals-cut-propagation Cross-port cut propagation

When @c BuilderOptions::propCutsAcrossPorts is true (the default), bit
boundaries introduced by an external concat are propagated inward across
the port so that the formal port and the module's internal assignments
keep the same per-bit granularity.

The mechanism is a @c CutRegistry side-table that maps each formal port's
internal @c ValueSymbol to a sorted set of bit offsets. It is populated
during Phase 1 and read during Phase 2:

- Before each @c InstanceSymbol body is visited,
  @c NetlistBuilder::recordCutsFromPortConnections walks every actual
  expression with @c collectActualCuts and registers the implied cut
  offsets against the corresponding formal port's internal symbol.
  Concatenation operand boundaries, replication unit boundaries, and the
  operand/padding boundary inside a widening conversion all contribute
  cuts. LSP-shaped operands also contribute any cuts already registered
  against their own root, so cuts flow down a multi-level hierarchy of
  pass-through ports.
- @c NetlistBuilder::handle(PortSymbol) calls
  @c materializePortNodes, which splits each driver's bit range at every
  registered cut and emits one @c Port node per segment instead of a
  single whole-port node.
- @c BitSliceList::build threads the registry into @c pushLsp;
  @c DataFlowAnalysis::handle(AssignmentExpression) supplies it. When an
  LSP's root has registered cuts intersecting the LSP's bounds,
  @c pushLsp emits a sub-slice per cut segment instead of a single slice
  covering the whole LSP. Each sub-slice keeps the full-LSP
  @c srcLo / @c srcHi so the offset math in
  @c driveLhsLspSegment / @c driveRhsLspSegment still recovers the
  correct LSP-internal bit.

Together, these give bit-precise paths through patterns such as
<tt>x ux(.x({b,a}), .y({d,c}))</tt> where the formal port, the
internal assignment, and the actual concat are all split at the same cut
offsets, and the resulting graph has no cross-bit edges.

The flag is only meaningful when @c resolveAssignBits is true. With cut
propagation off, port nodes and module-internal assignments stay
whole-word at port boundaries, matching the behaviour of releases
before this feature landed; the CLI exposes this as
@c --no-prop-cuts-across-ports.

@subsection internals-canonical-bodies Multi-instantiated modules

Slang's elaborator deduplicates equivalent instance bodies through
@c InstanceCacheKey: when two instances of the same module are
structurally identical (same parameters, ports, and members in the
same order), the second instance's @c InstanceBodySymbol is marked
non-canonical and points at the first via @c setCanonicalBody.
@c AnalysisManager::getDrivers then stores drivers only against the
canonical body's value symbols, so querying a non-canonical body
returns an empty driver list. With no compensation, only one instance
of a multi-instantiated module would receive any port-node or
internal-assignment connectivity.

@c NetlistBuilder always routes driver lookups for non-canonical
bodies to the corresponding symbol in the canonical body, so each
instance gets its own port nodes, assignment nodes, and per-bit
routing. Two memoized maps drive this:

- @c canonicalValueCache maps a value symbol from a non-canonical
  body to the corresponding value symbol in the canonical body.
  @c materializePortNodes and @c handle(VariableSymbol) consult this
  before calling @c AnalysisManager::getDrivers.
- @c canonicalBodyCache maps an instance body to its canonical
  counterpart. Unlike slang's @c getCanonicalBody, which only
  populates the outermost non-canonical instance, this cache covers
  every nested body too.

Both caches are populated lazily by @c getCanonicalBody, which walks up
the body chain looking for an <em>anchor</em>, a body whose canonical
is already known, either from slang's @c setCanonicalBody or from a
previous call. From the anchor, @c populatePairedBodies traverses the
anchor and its canonical in lockstep, recording every paired
@c ValueSymbol in @c canonicalValueCache and every paired
@c InstanceBodySymbol in @c canonicalBodyCache. The walk recurses
through @c GenerateBlockSymbol, @c GenerateBlockArraySymbol,
@c InstanceArraySymbol, and nested instance bodies, so a single
anchor lookup populates the entire subtree in one pass.

Positional matching is sound because @c InstanceCacheKey requires
identical content (parameters, ports, members in the same order)
before linking a canonical body. Nested non-canonical bodies are not
given a direct canonical pointer by slang. Once @c tryApplyFromCache
returns true on the outer instance, the visitor stops descending,
so this structural pairing is the only way to discover the deeper
correspondences.

Both caches are populated only during Phase 1's sequential AST
traversal; Phase 2's parallel DFA does not touch them, so they need
no synchronization. The caches are cleared between builds.

This pairing is unconditional: per-instance routing is the default
behaviour, since silently dropping every non-canonical instance was
unsound. The cost is that @c materializePortNodes creates one set of
port nodes per instance of every multi-instantiated module, and
shared signals (e.g. a clock or reset) drive each instance via its
own edge. To keep that affordable, @c DirectedGraph::getOrAddEdge finds the
existing edge through a per-node @c outEdgeIndex map once a node's
out-degree exceeds @c outEdgeIndexThreshold, so high-fan-out drivers
stay close to O(1) amortised per added edge instead of degrading to
quadratic.

@subsection internals-black-boxes Black-boxed instances

@c BuilderOptions::blackBoxes carries a list of glob patterns matched
against each @c InstanceSymbol's definition name and against its
hierarchical instance path; a single match flags the instance as a
black box. Matching is implemented by @c slang::netlist::wildcardMatch
in @c include/common/Wildcard.hpp (also used by the @c --find CLI flag
and the @c --scope/@c --name filters) and supports @c *, @c **, @c ...,
and @c ? (see @ref glob-syntax in the user guide for the full syntax).
The CLI exposes this via @c --black-box (repeatable) and the Python
bindings accept it as a @c black_boxes keyword argument on
@c NetlistGraph.build.

In @c NetlistBuilder::handle(InstanceSymbol const&), a matched
instance still has its formal port nodes materialised via
@c PortConnectionHandler::materializePortNodes, since the parent's port
wiring needs somewhere to terminate, but @c symbol.body.visit(*this)
is skipped, so no internal variables, assignments, procedural blocks,
or sub-instances of the matched module contribute to the graph. The
external port-connection assignments are still emitted, so fan-in and
fan-out queries terminate cleanly at the black-box boundary.

Cross-port cut propagation still runs first, so a concat-shaped
actual against a black-boxed instance produces the same per-bit
formal port nodes it would for a fully-elaborated instance.

Each matched instance's resolved hierarchical path is recorded on the
graph via @c NetlistGraph::addBlackBoxPath. This converts the
pattern-world (globs over definition names and paths) into concrete
instance paths at the only point where both are known, so subsequent
queries need neither the AST nor the original patterns.
@c NetlistGraph::getBlackBoxCoverage classifies any node against the
recorded paths by segment-aware prefix matching on its hierarchical
path: a Port node directly below a black-boxed instance is on the
boundary (@c BlackBoxCoverage::Boundary), any other node at or below
one is contained (@c Contained), and everything else, including nodes
without a hierarchical path, is outside (@c Outside). Containment
takes precedence over the boundary when boxes nest. The paths are
serialised in the JSON format (the @c blackBoxes field), so coverage
queries also work on deserialised graphs.

@subsection internals-operations Operation nodes

With @c BuilderOptions::expandOperations set (@c --expand-operations on the
CLI, @c expand_operations on @c NetlistGraph.build), the right-hand side of
an assignment is walked by @c OperationLowering, which creates one
@c Operation node per binary, unary or conditional operator. An
@c Operation records a netlist-local @c OperationKind together with the
result's width and signedness; it names the operator but carries nothing
with which to evaluate it.

What this buys a query is names along the path. Given

@code{.sv}
module m(input logic [7:0] a, input logic [7:0] b, input logic [7:0] c,
         output logic [7:0] y);
  assign y = (a & b) | c;
endmodule
@endcode

the default path reports the whole right-hand side as one step:

@code{.ansi}
$ slang-netlist ops.sv --from m.a --to m.y
ops.sv:3:10: note: assignment
  assign y = (a & b) | c;
         ^
@endcode

while with expansion each operator appears, located at its own token:

@code{.ansi}
$ slang-netlist ops.sv --expand-operations --from m.a --to m.y
ops.sv:3:17: note: operation & (BitwiseAnd, unsigned 8-bit)
  assign y = (a & b) | c;
                ^
ops.sv:3:22: note: operation | (BitwiseOr, unsigned 8-bit)
  assign y = (a & b) | c;
                     ^
ops.sv:3:10: note: assignment
  assign y = (a & b) | c;
         ^
@endcode

@c OperationKind is deliberately independent of @c ast::BinaryOperator and
@c ast::UnaryOperator: the serialised graph format is versioned and must
stay stable across slang upgrades, so it cannot inherit slang's enumerator
numbering. @c mapBinaryOperator and @c mapUnaryOperator are exhaustive
switches with no @c default arm.

@subsubsection internals-operations-shape Graph shape

Operators sit upstream of the per-segment @c Assignment node rather than
replacing it, so the assignment still stands for the write and the
operators describe how its value was computed:

@verbatim
  Op BitwiseAnd [8]  <-- a, b
      |
      v
  Op BitwiseOr [8]   <-- c
      |
      v
  Assignment
      |
      v
  y
@endverbatim

Edges between operators carry no symbol or bit range, since no named value
exists at that point in the expression. Only the edges into an operator
from leaf references, and the edge out of the @c Assignment, are
annotated, which @c --netlist-dot shows directly (edges grouped here by
kind rather than in emission order):

@code{.ansi}
N1 -> N7 [label="a[7:0]"]    // leaf references, annotated
N2 -> N7 [label="b[7:0]"]
N3 -> N6 [label="c[7:0]"]
N7 -> N6                     // Op BitwiseAnd -> Op BitwiseOr, bare
N6 -> N5                     // Op BitwiseOr -> Assignment, bare
N5 -> N4 [label="y[7:0]"]    // the write, annotated
@endcode

@subsubsection internals-operations-mechanism The lowering mechanism

The mechanism is a retarget of the analysis's current node. R-value edges
land on <tt>getState().node</tt>, so lowering an operator is: create the
node, add an edge from it to the previous current node, set
<tt>getState().node</tt> to it, visit the operands, and restore. Leaf
references then attach to the correct operator with no change to
@c noteReference or any other handler. Walking the same right-hand side:

@verbatim
  visitOperand((a & b) | c)    current = Assignment
    create Op BitwiseOr        emit  Op| -> Assignment
    current = Op BitwiseOr
    visitOperand(a & b)        current = Op BitwiseOr
      create Op BitwiseAnd     emit  Op& -> Op|
      current = Op BitwiseAnd
      visitOperand(a)          leaf, emit  a -> Op&
      visitOperand(b)          leaf, emit  b -> Op&
      restore                  current = Op BitwiseOr
    visitOperand(c)            leaf, emit  c -> Op|
    restore                    current = Assignment
@endverbatim

The restore on exit is what keeps sibling operands independent: an
assignment nested in one operand, such as a function call's output
argument, retargets the current node, and without the restore the
following operand would attach to whatever that left behind.

The current node is set directly and never through @c updateNode, which
would also wire an edge from the enclosing condition and so invent a
dependency from that condition to every operand.

An @c Operation's source location is the operator token
(@c BinaryExpression::opRange / @c UnaryExpression::opRange) for binary and
unary operators, which is what puts the carets in the path output above on
the @c & and the @c | rather than both on the whole expression. A @c \?:
has no single operator token, so its node is located at the whole
conditional expression instead.

@subsubsection internals-operations-conditionals Conditional operators

A top-level @c \?: in an assignment is never expanded. Slang binds a
conditional's narrow arm as a width-extending conversion so that all three
widths agree, and @c BitSliceList::build decomposes such a conditional into
per-arm slices before lowering ever sees it as an expression to classify,
see @ref internals-bit-aligned. So <tt>assign y = c ? a : b;</tt> yields no
@c Operation node at all, regardless of arm widths:

@verbatim
  Assignment  <-- c, a, b
      |
      v
  y
@endverbatim

Only a @c \?: nested under another operator becomes an @c Operation node,
because lowering recurses into operands directly and bypasses
@c BitSliceList. For <tt>assign y = (c ? a : b) & mask;</tt>:

@verbatim
  Op Conditional [8]  <-- c, a, b
      |
      v
  Op BitwiseAnd [8]   <-- mask
      |
      v
  Assignment
      |
      v
  y
@endverbatim

Operands that are only conditionally evaluated, namely a conditional's
arms and the right operand of a short-circuiting @c && , @c || or @c ->,
are handed back to the flow analysis rather than walked here. It splits
the reaching definitions across such an operand and rejoins afterwards;
walking it directly would let a write in one branch kill the definitions
reaching the other instead of merging with them, dropping a real
dependency. Leaf references still attach to the operator node, since it is
the current one throughout, so the operator itself is expanded as usual.

The cost is that operators nested inside such an operand stay opaque, as
the flow analysis dispatches them through the ordinary expression visitor.
Both of these therefore produce the graph above, with no node for the
@c & or the @c | inside the arms:

@code{.sv}
assign y = (c ? a : b) & mask;
assign y = (c ? (a & b) : (a | b)) & mask;
@endcode

Enabling expansion therefore never drops a dependency that the default path
records, and on the whole only adds operator nodes along paths that already
existed. It can still add a path where the default path has a residual gap:
when a conditional's arm contains an assignment the arms stop sharing a
current node, and a reference read after the conditional loses its
dependency, which lowering records because it walks the operands itself.
So <tt>y = (c ? (t = b) : t) & mask;</tt> gains an edge from @c mask. That
is a known gap in the default path rather than an over-approximation here.

@subsubsection internals-operations-cost Cost

Expansion is per (segment, expression): where cut propagation has split the
left-hand side, each segment's opaque source is lowered separately and gets
its own operator subtree. For <tt>assign {p, q} = a & b;</tt> with 4-bit
@c p and @c q, the one @c & in the source becomes two nodes:

@verbatim
  Op BitwiseAnd [8]  <-- a, b
      |
      v
  Assignment
      |
      v
  p

  Op BitwiseAnd [8]  <-- a, b
      |
      v
  Assignment
      |
      v
  q
@endverbatim

@c p takes the high four bits of <tt>a & b</tt> and @c q the low four, but
both operator nodes cover the full 8-bit width of the expression, since
that is its type; it is the assignment each feeds that is narrow, and the
edge out of it is annotated with that symbol's own range (@c p[3:0] and
@c q[3:0]). Node count from expansion therefore scales with the number of
cut segments times the number of operators in the expression, which is the
main reason the option is off by default.

Increment and decrement operators mutate their operand and are left
opaque, as are operators whose result type is not integral, conditionals
bearing a pattern or more than one condition, function calls, streaming
concatenations and non-constant selects.

@subsection internals-limitations Known limitations

The bit-aligned path falls back to @c handleAssignmentLegacy /
@c handlePortConnectionLegacy in three situations:

- Either side is non-integral. Strings, unpacked aggregates, and other
  dynamically-sized types do not have a well-defined selectable width, so
  no slicelist is built and the legacy LSP visitor walks the expression and
  records edges over the full LSP bounds.
- The two slicelists disagree on total width. Slang's type-checking is
  lenient enough that the LHS and RHS can have different selectable widths
  even when both are integral (e.g. an enum-to-logic coercion at a port
  connection re-parented into the assignment, or an instance-array port
  implicitly sliced per instance). The fallback is bit-imprecise but
  always sound.
- @c resolveAssignBits is false. The CLI exposes this as
  @c --no-resolve-assign-bits and the Python bindings as the
  @c resolve_assign_bits keyword argument to @c NetlistGraph.build. With
  the flag off, @c BitSliceList::build returns a single opaque slice
  covering the whole expression, so every LSP on one side of an assignment
  fans into every LSP on the other side. This matches the behaviour of
  releases before bit-aligned resolution landed.

Beyond the fallbacks, the structural decomposition itself does not resolve
operators bit by bit. Any expression that is not a concatenation,
replication, equal-width @c \?:, or width-changing conversion is one opaque
slice, and every LSP found inside it fans into the full bit range of that
slice. <tt>y = a & b</tt> is still not resolved bit-by-bit: bit 0 of @c y
is recorded as depending on every bit of @c a and @c b. The same applies to
other bitwise, arithmetic, relational, reduction, streaming-concatenation,
function-call, non-constant select, and pattern-bearing conditional
expressions.

With @c expandOperations set, such an expression is no longer @e opaque:
the operators inside it become @c Operation nodes, so a traced path names
them (see @ref internals-operations). The bit-level fan-in is unchanged, as
operator nodes cover the full width of the slice they serve and are not
split into per-bit gates.

In every fallback or opaque case the resulting graph is still sound, as no
real dependency is missed, but queries over the affected nodes may report
paths that a deeper Boolean analysis could rule out.

*/
